/*
 * Copyright 2026 NXP
 * SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
 *
 * System-PM constraint layer: mark which SoC power states stop this CTIMER
 * counting. Stacked on top of whatever the board target already applies, for the
 * system layer only (via EXTRA_DTC_OVERLAY_FILE).
 *
 * Why all three, and why this belongs here rather than in the SoC dtsi. Per MCX
 * Nx4x RM Rev. 6_RC2 Table 336, CTIMER0 is "LP/Static" in both Deep Sleep and Power
 * Down and CTIMER1-3 are "LP/Static" in Deep Sleep -- that is, the block *can* keep
 * counting in those states, but only on a functional clock that survives them. The
 * MCXN and MCXA boards this case runs on all attach PLL0 (or the equivalent
 * system PLL) to their CTIMER, and that PLL is off in Deep Sleep, so on these boards
 * every one of the three states does stop the counter. Which clock feeds a CTIMER is
 * a board decision, not an SoC one, so the SoC dtsi cannot declare this list for
 * every board the way the DAC nodes can -- a board that attached the low-power
 * oscillator instead would be declaring a constraint it does not have.
 *
 * Deep Power Down additionally gates the CORE domain and resets the registers, which
 * is the stronger loss the dpd layer is about.
 */

&ctimer0 {
	zephyr,disabling-power-states = <&deepsleep &powerdown &deeppowerdown>;
};
